IMPORT prelude

-- Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Commonwealth of Australia) -- which court can hear a civil claim that
-- crosses the line between federal and State or Territory jurisdiction (s 4); when a proceeding must or may be moved to
-- another court (ss 5, 6, 6A, 8, 10); where an appeal must be brought (s 7); which law the court applies (s 11); what happens
-- to the steps and costs already taken (ss 11(3), 12); no appeal from a transfer decision (s 13); and enforcing the judgment
-- (s 14).
--
-- SEAT: the party to a civil proceeding -- usually the claimant who brings it, sometimes the defendant who answers it (an
-- intervener counts: s 3(1) "party ... includes a person who intervenes"). "I have filed my claim in the Supreme Court. Can it
-- hear the federal part? Must my case be sent to the Federal Court? Where do I appeal? Can I appeal the transfer?" The Act's
-- provisions are addressed to the courts; they are encoded as what they mean for the party. Every opinion the Act leaves to a
-- court ("it appears to the first court", "the interests of justice", "special reasons", "considers it appropriate") is asked
-- as an input, never decided.
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 23 (compilation date 6 February 2023, includes amendments up to Act No. 79, 2022), registered as
-- C2023C00038, Federal Register of Legislation, retrieved 13 October 2026, as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A03433.txt. No instrument sets a number or date this Act uses.
--
-- NOT ENCODED (machinery or not addressed to a party): ss 1, 2 (title, commencement); s 3(3), (4) (external Territories; ACT
-- and NT application laws treated as federal jurisdiction); s 6(6) (what the court weighs on "special reasons": an opinion,
-- asked as an input); s 6A(4), (5) (no new jurisdiction; reading "interests of justice"); s 9 (the courts' capacity to
-- exercise cross-vested jurisdiction); s 14(3) (references in other laws); s 15 (reading down); s 16 (suspension by
-- Proclamation). State and Territory cross-vesting Acts, which give the Federal Court State jurisdiction, are a different
-- law and are not encoded.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the party's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 -- the party to a civil proceeding`

§§ `Questions this encoding does not cover`

-- The stable l4 has no REFUSE. A question that can reach a gap answers with an `Answer`: either `answered` with the value,
-- or `not covered` with the reason.
DECLARE `Why not covered` IS ONE OF
    `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`
    `the person is not a party to the proceeding (a party includes an intervener, s 3(1)); this Act gives a non-party no application`
    `the court is not exercising jurisdiction conferred by this Act or by a State cross-vesting law, so s 11 does not say which law it applies`
    `this Act does not say where this appeal lies: that is for the Act that created the court`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered`    HAS `the answer` IS AN a
    `not covered` HAS `because`    IS A `Why not covered`

§§ `The courts`

-- s 3(1): "State includes the Australian Capital Territory and the Northern Territory"; "Territory does not include the
-- Australian Capital Territory or the Northern Territory" (so a Territory is an external Territory, such as Norfolk Island,
-- Christmas Island or the Cocos (Keeling) Islands); "Federal Court means the Federal Court of Australia"; "State Family Court,
-- in relation to a State, means a court of that State to which section 41 of the Family Law Act 1975 applies by virtue of a
-- Proclamation made under subsection 41(2) of that Act" (the Family Court of Western Australia).
-- s 3(2): "A reference in this Act, other than a reference in subsection 4(1), 5(3) or 7(4), to the Supreme Court of a State
-- includes, if there is a State Family Court of that State, a reference to that State Family Court."
DECLARE `Court` IS ONE OF
    `the Federal Court of Australia`
    `the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1)`
    `the Supreme Court of one of the six States`
    `the Supreme Court of the Australian Capital Territory or the Northern Territory`
    `the Supreme Court of an external Territory`
    `a State Family Court (the Family Court of Western Australia)`
    `a lower court or tribunal of an external Territory`
    `another court or tribunal`

GIVEN c IS A `Court`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is one of the two federal courts` c IF
        c EQUALS `the Federal Court of Australia`
    OR  c EQUALS `the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1)`

-- "the Supreme Court of a State or Territory": a State includes the ACT and the NT, and a Territory is an external Territory.
GIVEN c IS A `Court`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a Supreme Court of a State or Territory` c IF
        c EQUALS `the Supreme Court of one of the six States`
    OR  c EQUALS `the Supreme Court of the Australian Capital Territory or the Northern Territory`
    OR  c EQUALS `the Supreme Court of an external Territory`

-- The same, read with s 3(2): a State Family Court is a Supreme Court of a State, except in ss 4(1), 5(3) and 7(4).
GIVEN c IS A `Court`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a Supreme Court of a State or Territory, read with s 3(2)` c IF
        `is a Supreme Court of a State or Territory` c
    OR  c EQUALS `a State Family Court (the Family Court of Western Australia)`

§§ `s 4 -- can this court hear my civil matter`

-- s 4(4): "This section does not apply to a matter arising under: (a) the Conciliation and Arbitration Act 1904; or (ab) the
-- Fair Work Act 2009; or (aba) the Federal Safety Commissioner Act 2022; or (ac) the Fair Work (Registered Organisations) Act
-- 2009; or (ad) the Fair Work (Transitional Provisions and Consequential Amendments) Act 2009; or (b) the Workplace Relations
-- Act 1996; or (ba) the Native Title Act 1993; or (c) section 45D, 45DA, 45DB, 45E, 45EA,46A, 155A or 155B of the Competition
-- and Consumer Act 2010; or (d) a provision of Part VI or XII of the Competition and Consumer Act 2010 so far as the provision
-- relates to section 46A, 155A or 155B of that Act."
DECLARE `Excluded law` IS ONE OF
    `none of the laws section 4(4) lists`
    `the Conciliation and Arbitration Act 1904`
    `the Fair Work Act 2009`
    `the Federal Safety Commissioner Act 2022`
    `the Fair Work (Registered Organisations) Act 2009`
    `the Fair Work (Transitional Provisions and Consequential Amendments) Act 2009`
    `the Workplace Relations Act 1996`
    `the Native Title Act 1993`
    `section 45D, 45DA, 45DB, 45E, 45EA, 46A, 155A or 155B of the Competition and Consumer Act 2010`
    `a provision of Part VI or XII of the Competition and Consumer Act 2010, so far as it relates to section 46A, 155A or 155B`

DECLARE `Can this court hear my matter` IS ONE OF
    `yes, without this Act: the court has jurisdiction over the matter apart from section 4`
    `yes: the State Supreme Court is invested with federal jurisdiction over the matter, because the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1) has jurisdiction over it (s 4(1)(c))`
    `yes: jurisdiction over the matter is conferred on the Territory Supreme Court, because the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1) has jurisdiction over it (s 4(1)(d))`
    `yes: jurisdiction over the matter is conferred on this court, because the Supreme Court of an external Territory has jurisdiction over it (s 4(2))`
    `yes: my proceeding was transferred to this court, which has jurisdiction over every matter in it that it would not otherwise have (s 4(3))`
    `no: section 4 does not apply to a matter arising under the law I named (s 4(4))`
    `no: section 4 confers jurisdiction over civil matters only, and a criminal proceeding is not a proceeding under this Act (s 3(1), s 4(1), (2))`
    `no: section 4 gives this court no jurisdiction over the matter on these facts`

-- s 4(1): "Where: (a) the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1) has jurisdiction
-- with respect to a civil matter ...; and (b) the Supreme Court of a State or Territory would not, apart from this section,
-- have jurisdiction with respect to that matter; then: (c) in the case of the Supreme Court of a State (other than the Supreme
-- Court of the Australian Capital Territory and the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory)--that court is invested with
-- federal jurisdiction with respect to that matter; or (d) in the case of the Supreme Court of a Territory (including the
-- Australian Capital Territory and the Northern Territory)--jurisdiction is conferred on that court with respect to that
-- matter." s 3(2) does not reach s 4(1): a State Family Court is not a Supreme Court there.
-- s 4(2): "Where: (a) the Supreme Court of a Territory has jurisdiction with respect to a civil matter ...; and (b) the
-- Federal Court, the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1) or the Supreme Court of a State or of another
-- Territory would not, apart from this section, have jurisdiction with respect to that matter; jurisdiction is conferred on
-- the court referred to in paragraph (b) with respect to that matter." (Here s 3(2) does apply: a State Family Court counts.)
-- s 4(3): "Where a proceeding is transferred to the Federal Court, the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division
-- 1) or a State Family Court of a State, that court has, by virtue of this subsection, jurisdiction with respect to so many of
-- the matters for determination in the proceeding as that court would not have apart from this subsection."
@export I have a civil claim, or I am defending one. Can the court where it is filed hear the matter, given that it may fall within federal jurisdiction or another court's jurisdiction?
GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court` @desc The court where your proceeding is filed or pending (a State "Supreme Court" for the ACT and the NT is listed separately; an external Territory is Norfolk Island, Christmas Island or the Cocos (Keeling) Islands)
      `the court has jurisdiction apart from section 4` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that court could hear the matter anyway, without this Act (for example a claim under the law of its own State)
      `it is a criminal proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding is a criminal prosecution (this Act covers civil proceedings only)
      `the law the matter arises under` IS AN `Excluded law` @desc Whether the matter arises under one of the laws section 4(4) lists (Fair Work, native title, some competition law provisions); choose "none" if it does not
      `the Federal Court or the FCFCOA has jurisdiction over the matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Federal Court of Australia or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1) has jurisdiction over the matter (for example a claim under a Commonwealth Act that gives that court jurisdiction)
      `an external Territory's Supreme Court has jurisdiction over the matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court of an external Territory (Norfolk Island, Christmas Island, Cocos (Keeling) Islands) has jurisdiction over the matter
      `my proceeding was transferred to this court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding came to this court by a transfer from another court
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can this court hear my matter`
DECIDE `Can this court hear my matter question` IS
    IF `the court` EQUALS `another court or tribunal` OR `the court` EQUALS `a lower court or tribunal of an external Territory`
    THEN `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`
    ELSE IF `the court has jurisdiction apart from section 4`
         THEN `answered` `yes, without this Act: the court has jurisdiction over the matter apart from section 4`
         ELSE IF `it is a criminal proceeding`
              THEN `answered` `no: section 4 confers jurisdiction over civil matters only, and a criminal proceeding is not a proceeding under this Act (s 3(1), s 4(1), (2))`
              ELSE IF (NOT (`the law the matter arises under` EQUALS `none of the laws section 4(4) lists`))
                   THEN `answered` `no: section 4 does not apply to a matter arising under the law I named (s 4(4))`
                   ELSE `answered` (`section 4 route` `the court` `the Federal Court or the FCFCOA has jurisdiction over the matter` `an external Territory's Supreme Court has jurisdiction over the matter` `my proceeding was transferred to this court`)

GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court`
      `federal jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
      `external Territory jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
      `transferred` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can this court hear my matter`
`section 4 route` `the court` `federal jurisdiction` `external Territory jurisdiction` `transferred` MEANS
    IF `transferred` AND (`is one of the two federal courts` `the court` OR `the court` EQUALS `a State Family Court (the Family Court of Western Australia)`)
    THEN `yes: my proceeding was transferred to this court, which has jurisdiction over every matter in it that it would not otherwise have (s 4(3))`
    ELSE IF `federal jurisdiction` AND `the court` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of one of the six States`
         THEN `yes: the State Supreme Court is invested with federal jurisdiction over the matter, because the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1) has jurisdiction over it (s 4(1)(c))`
         ELSE IF `federal jurisdiction` AND (`the court` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of the Australian Capital Territory or the Northern Territory` OR `the court` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of an external Territory`)
              THEN `yes: jurisdiction over the matter is conferred on the Territory Supreme Court, because the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1) has jurisdiction over it (s 4(1)(d))`
              ELSE IF `external Territory jurisdiction` AND (`is one of the two federal courts` `the court` OR `is a Supreme Court of a State or Territory, read with s 3(2)` `the court`)
                   THEN `yes: jurisdiction over the matter is conferred on this court, because the Supreme Court of an external Territory has jurisdiction over it (s 4(2))`
                   ELSE `no: section 4 gives this court no jurisdiction over the matter on these facts`

§§ `s 5 -- must my proceeding be transferred to another court`

-- s 5(1) (Supreme Court to the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1)), (2) (Supreme Court to the Supreme Court of another
-- State or Territory), (3) (between the Supreme Court of a State and the State Family Court of that State), (4) (Federal
-- Court or FCFCOA (Division 1) to a Supreme Court), (5) (between the Federal Court and the FCFCOA (Division 1)). Each says
-- that where the proceeding is pending in the first court "and it appears to the first court that: (i) the relevant
-- proceeding arises out of, or is related to, another proceeding pending in [the other court] and it is more appropriate that
-- the relevant proceeding be determined by [it]; (ii) having regard to [the matters listed] it is more appropriate that the
-- relevant proceeding be determined by [it]; or (iii) it is otherwise in the interests of justice that the relevant
-- proceeding be determined by [it]; the first court shall transfer the relevant proceeding to [it]." In s 5(3) the list in
-- (ii) is shorter, and in s 5(5) there is no list: (ii) there is "otherwise in the interests of justice". "Supreme Court of a
-- State" includes the State Family Court in s 5(1), (2) and (4), but not in s 5(3) (s 3(2)).
-- Note to s 5: "This section has effect subject to section 6 ... and section 6A".
-- s 5(9): "Nothing in this section confers on a court jurisdiction that the court would not otherwise have."
DECLARE `Destination` IS ONE OF
    `the Federal Court of Australia`
    `the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1)`
    `the Supreme Court of a (different) State or Territory`
    `the Supreme Court of the same State`
    `the State Family Court of the same State`

DECLARE `Must my proceeding be transferred` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court must transfer it to the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1) (s 5(1))`
    `yes: the court must transfer it to that other Supreme Court (s 5(2))`
    `yes: the court must transfer it to the other of the State's Supreme Court and State Family Court (s 5(3))`
    `yes: the court must transfer it to that Supreme Court (s 5(4))`
    `yes: the court must transfer it to the other of the Federal Court and the FCFCOA (Division 1) (s 5(5))`
    `no: none of the grounds in section 5 appears to the court, so section 5 does not require a transfer`
    `section 6 governs instead: my proceeding in a Supreme Court involves a special federal matter`
    `not required: section 6A applies, so neither s 5(1) nor s 6 requires the Supreme Court to transfer my federal matter proceeding; it may still do so if it considers that appropriate (s 6A(3))`
    `not required: section 6A applies, so s 5(4) does not apply; the federal court may transfer my federal matter proceeding to the Supreme Court of the State if it considers that appropriate (s 6A(2))`

GIVEN `from` IS A `Court`
      `to` IS A `Destination`
GIVETH A NUMBER
-- Which subsection of s 5 joins the two courts (0: none).
`s 5 subsection for` `from` `to` MEANS
    IF `is a Supreme Court of a State or Territory, read with s 3(2)` `from` AND (`to` EQUALS `the Federal Court of Australia` OR `to` EQUALS `the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1)`)
    THEN 1
    ELSE IF `is a Supreme Court of a State or Territory, read with s 3(2)` `from` AND `to` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of a (different) State or Territory`
         THEN 2
         ELSE IF (`from` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` OR `from` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of the Australian Capital Territory or the Northern Territory`) AND `to` EQUALS `the State Family Court of the same State`
              THEN 3
              ELSE IF `from` EQUALS `a State Family Court (the Family Court of Western Australia)` AND `to` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of the same State`
                   THEN 3
                   ELSE IF `is one of the two federal courts` `from` AND (`to` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of a (different) State or Territory` OR `to` EQUALS `the State Family Court of the same State`)
                        THEN 4
                        ELSE IF `from` EQUALS `the Federal Court of Australia` AND `to` EQUALS `the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1)`
                             THEN 5
                             ELSE IF `from` EQUALS `the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1)` AND `to` EQUALS `the Federal Court of Australia`
                                  THEN 5
                                  ELSE 0

@export My civil proceeding is in one court and could be heard in another. Must the court transfer it to the other court?
GIVEN `the court my proceeding is in` IS A `Court` @desc The court where your proceeding is pending now
      `the other court` IS A `Destination` @desc The court it might go to ("a different State or Territory" means a Supreme Court other than the one your proceeding is in, and for a proceeding in the Federal Court or the FCFCOA any Supreme Court; "the same State" means the Supreme Court or the State Family Court of the State your proceeding is in, and for a proceeding in a federal court choose the State Family Court for the Family Court of Western Australia)
      `a related proceeding is pending in the other court and the court thinks it more appropriate that the other court decide mine` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding arises out of, or is related to, another proceeding pending in the other court, and it appears to your court more appropriate that the other court decide yours (s 5, ground (i))
      `having regard to the listed matters, the court thinks it more appropriate that the other court decide mine` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, weighing where the case could have been started without this Act, how far it turns on the other court's law, and the interests of justice, it appears to your court more appropriate that the other court decide it (s 5, ground (ii); not available between the Federal Court and the FCFCOA)
      `the court thinks it otherwise in the interests of justice that the other court decide mine` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to your court that it is otherwise in the interests of justice that the other court decide it (s 5, ground (iii))
      `my proceeding in a Supreme Court involves a special federal matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a matter in your proceeding is a "special federal matter" (see the section 6 question: for example a competition claim under Part IV of the Competition and Consumer Act 2010, or judicial review under the ADJR Act); only matters for a proceeding in a Supreme Court
      `section 6A applies to my proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether section 6A applies (see the section 6A question): your proceeding is federal judicial review of a Commonwealth body acting under a State law, related to a State proceeding about that State law
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Must my proceeding be transferred`
DECIDE `Must my proceeding be transferred question` IS
    IF `section 6A applies to my proceeding` AND `is one of the two federal courts` `the court my proceeding is in` AND `the other court` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of a (different) State or Territory`
    THEN `answered` `not required: section 6A applies, so s 5(4) does not apply; the federal court may transfer my federal matter proceeding to the Supreme Court of the State if it considers that appropriate (s 6A(2))`
    ELSE IF `section 6A applies to my proceeding` AND `the court my proceeding is in` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` AND (`the other court` EQUALS `the Federal Court of Australia` OR `the other court` EQUALS `the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1)`)
         THEN `answered` `not required: section 6A applies, so neither s 5(1) nor s 6 requires the Supreme Court to transfer my federal matter proceeding; it may still do so if it considers that appropriate (s 6A(3))`
         ELSE IF `my proceeding in a Supreme Court involves a special federal matter` AND `is a Supreme Court of a State or Territory, read with s 3(2)` `the court my proceeding is in`
              THEN `answered` `section 6 governs instead: my proceeding in a Supreme Court involves a special federal matter`
              ELSE `s 5 answer` (`s 5 subsection for` `the court my proceeding is in` `the other court`) (`a related proceeding is pending in the other court and the court thinks it more appropriate that the other court decide mine` OR `the court thinks it otherwise in the interests of justice that the other court decide mine`) `having regard to the listed matters, the court thinks it more appropriate that the other court decide mine`

GIVEN `subsection` IS A NUMBER
      `ground (i) or (iii)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `ground (ii)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Must my proceeding be transferred`
`s 5 answer` `subsection` `ground (i) or (iii)` `ground (ii)` MEANS
    IF `subsection` EQUALS 0
    THEN `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`
    ELSE IF (NOT (`ground (i) or (iii)` OR (`ground (ii)` AND (NOT (`subsection` EQUALS 5)))))
         THEN `answered` `no: none of the grounds in section 5 appears to the court, so section 5 does not require a transfer`
         ELSE IF `subsection` EQUALS 1
              THEN `answered` `yes: the court must transfer it to the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1) (s 5(1))`
              ELSE IF `subsection` EQUALS 2
                   THEN `answered` `yes: the court must transfer it to that other Supreme Court (s 5(2))`
                   ELSE IF `subsection` EQUALS 3
                        THEN `answered` `yes: the court must transfer it to the other of the State's Supreme Court and State Family Court (s 5(3))`
                        ELSE IF `subsection` EQUALS 4
                             THEN `answered` `yes: the court must transfer it to that Supreme Court (s 5(4))`
                             ELSE `answered` `yes: the court must transfer it to the other of the Federal Court and the FCFCOA (Division 1) (s 5(5))`

-- s 5(6): "Where: (a) a court ... transfers a proceeding to another court under a law or laws relating to cross-vesting of
-- jurisdiction; and (b) it appears to the first court that: (i) there is another proceeding pending in the first court that
-- arises out of, or is related to, the first-mentioned proceeding; and (ii) it is in the interests of justice that the other
-- proceeding be determined by the other court; the first court shall transfer the other proceeding to the other court."
DECLARE `Must my related proceeding follow` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court must transfer my related proceeding to the same court (s 5(6))`
    `no: section 5(6) requires it only where the court has transferred the first proceeding under a cross-vesting law and it appears to the court that my related proceeding should, in the interests of justice, be decided by the other court`

@export The court has transferred one proceeding to another court under the cross-vesting laws. Must my related proceeding in the same court go with it?
GIVEN `the court transferred the first proceeding under a cross-vesting law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court transferred the first proceeding to another court under this Act or a State or Territory cross-vesting Act
      `my proceeding is pending in the same court and arises out of or is related to it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is pending in that same court and arises out of, or is related to, the transferred one
      `the court thinks the interests of justice favour the other court deciding mine` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the court that it is in the interests of justice that your proceeding be decided by the court the first one went to
GIVETH A `Must my related proceeding follow`
DECIDE `Must my related proceeding follow question` IS
    IF `the court transferred the first proceeding under a cross-vesting law` AND `my proceeding is pending in the same court and arises out of or is related to it` AND `the court thinks the interests of justice favour the other court deciding mine`
    THEN `yes: the court must transfer my related proceeding to the same court (s 5(6))`
    ELSE `no: section 5(6) requires it only where the court has transferred the first proceeding under a cross-vesting law and it appears to the court that my related proceeding should, in the interests of justice, be decided by the other court`

-- s 5(7): "A court may transfer a proceeding under this section on the application of a party to the proceeding, of its own
-- motion or on the application of the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth or of a State or Territory." s 8(1) (removal to a
-- Territory Supreme Court) and s 10 (transfer of an Australian Consumer Law matter) also run "on the application of a party
-- to the ... proceeding or of its own motion". s 3(1): "party, in relation to a proceeding, includes a person who intervenes
-- in the proceeding."
DECLARE `Can I apply for a transfer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: as a party (an intervener counts) I may apply; the court may also act of its own motion, and under section 5 the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth or of a State or Territory may apply (ss 3(1), 5(7), 8(1), 10)`

@export Can I ask the court to transfer my proceeding to another court under this Act?
GIVEN `I am a party to the proceeding or have intervened in it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a party to the proceeding, including as an intervener
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can I apply for a transfer`
DECIDE `Can I apply for a transfer question` IS
    IF `I am a party to the proceeding or have intervened in it`
    THEN `answered` `yes: as a party (an intervener counts) I may apply; the court may also act of its own motion, and under section 5 the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth or of a State or Territory may apply (ss 3(1), 5(7), 8(1), 10)`
    ELSE `not covered` `the person is not a party to the proceeding (a party includes an intervener, s 3(1)); this Act gives a non-party no application`

-- s 5(8): a person "entitled to practise as a barrister or a solicitor ... in a court has, if a proceeding ... in that court is
-- transferred to another court under a law or laws relating to cross-vesting of jurisdiction, the same entitlement to
-- practise in relation to: (a) the transferred proceeding; and (b) any other proceeding out of which the transferred
-- proceeding arises or to which the transferred proceeding is related, being another proceeding that is to be determined
-- together with the transferred proceeding; in the other court that the person would have if the other court were a federal
-- court exercising federal jurisdiction."
DECLARE `Can my lawyer keep acting` IS ONE OF
    `yes: my lawyer has the same right to practise in the new court, for my transferred proceeding and any related proceeding decided with it, as in a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction (s 5(8))`
    `no: section 5(8) carries over only a right to practise that my lawyer had in the court the proceeding came from, and only on a transfer under a cross-vesting law`

@export My proceeding has been transferred to a court in another State or to a federal court. Can my lawyer keep acting for me there?
GIVEN `my lawyer may practise in the court my proceeding came from` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your barrister or solicitor is entitled to practise in the court where the proceeding was before the transfer
      `it was transferred under a cross-vesting law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the transfer was made under this Act or a State or Territory cross-vesting Act
GIVETH A `Can my lawyer keep acting`
DECIDE `Can my lawyer keep acting question` IS
    IF `my lawyer may practise in the court my proceeding came from` AND `it was transferred under a cross-vesting law`
    THEN `yes: my lawyer has the same right to practise in the new court, for my transferred proceeding and any related proceeding decided with it, as in a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction (s 5(8))`
    ELSE `no: section 5(8) carries over only a right to practise that my lawyer had in the court the proceeding came from, and only on a transfer under a cross-vesting law`

§§ `s 6 -- special federal matters in a Supreme Court`

-- s 3(1) "special federal matter means: (a) a matter arising under Part IV of the Competition and Consumer Act 2010 (other
-- than under section 45D, 45DA, 45DB, 45E or 45EA); or (aa) a matter arising under the Competition Code ... of the Australian
-- Capital Territory or the Northern Territory; or (ab) a matter arising under section 60G of the Family Law Act 1975 in a
-- court other than the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory; or (b) a matter
-- involving the determination of questions of law on appeal from a decision of, or of questions of law referred or stated by,
-- a tribunal or other body established by an Act or a person holding office under an Act, not being a matter for
-- determination in an appeal or a reference or case stated to the Supreme Court of a State or Territory under a law of the
-- Commonwealth that specifically provides for such an appeal, reference or case stated to such a court; or (c) a matter
-- arising under the Administrative Decisions (Judicial Review) Act 1977; or (e) a matter that is within the original
-- jurisdiction of the Federal Court by virtue of section 39B of the Judiciary Act 1903; being a matter in respect of which the
-- Supreme Court of a State or Territory would not, apart from this Act, have jurisdiction." There is no paragraph (d).
DECLARE `Kind of matter` IS ONE OF
    `(a) Part IV of the Competition and Consumer Act 2010 (restrictive trade practices), other than s 45D, 45DA, 45DB, 45E or 45EA`
    `(aa) the Competition Code of the Australian Capital Territory or the Northern Territory`
    `(ab) section 60G of the Family Law Act 1975 (leave for a prescribed adoption)`
    `(b) questions of law on appeal from, or referred or stated by, a Commonwealth tribunal, body or office-holder`
    `(c) the Administrative Decisions (Judicial Review) Act 1977`
    `(e) the Federal Court's original jurisdiction under section 39B of the Judiciary Act 1903`
    `none of these`

GIVEN `kind` IS A `Kind of matter`
      `my proceeding is in the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a Commonwealth law specifically provides for this appeal, reference or case stated to a Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Supreme Court would have jurisdiction apart from this Act` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a special federal matter` `kind` `my proceeding is in the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory` `a Commonwealth law specifically provides for this appeal, reference or case stated to a Supreme Court` `the Supreme Court would have jurisdiction apart from this Act` IF
        (NOT `the Supreme Court would have jurisdiction apart from this Act`)
    AND (   `kind` EQUALS `(a) Part IV of the Competition and Consumer Act 2010 (restrictive trade practices), other than s 45D, 45DA, 45DB, 45E or 45EA`
         OR `kind` EQUALS `(aa) the Competition Code of the Australian Capital Territory or the Northern Territory`
         OR (`kind` EQUALS `(ab) section 60G of the Family Law Act 1975 (leave for a prescribed adoption)` AND (NOT `my proceeding is in the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory`))
         OR (`kind` EQUALS `(b) questions of law on appeal from, or referred or stated by, a Commonwealth tribunal, body or office-holder` AND (NOT `a Commonwealth law specifically provides for this appeal, reference or case stated to a Supreme Court`))
         OR `kind` EQUALS `(c) the Administrative Decisions (Judicial Review) Act 1977`
         OR `kind` EQUALS `(e) the Federal Court's original jurisdiction under section 39B of the Judiciary Act 1903`)

-- s 6(1): "If: (a) a matter for determination in a proceeding that is pending in the Supreme Court of a State or Territory is a
-- special federal matter; and (b) the court does not make an order under subsection (3) in respect of the matter; the court
-- must transfer the proceeding in accordance with this section to the Federal Court or a court mentioned in paragraph (2)(b)."
-- (1A): "However, the court must only transfer so much of the proceeding as is, in the opinion of the court, within the
-- jurisdiction (including the accrued jurisdiction) of the Federal Court, or the court mentioned in paragraph (2)(b) ..."
-- (2): to the Federal Court for paragraphs (a), (b), (c) or (e) [the text omits (aa); see NOTES.md]; for (ab) "to whichever of
-- the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1), the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court
-- of the Northern Territory, in the opinion of the court, is appropriate in the circumstances."
-- (3): "The Supreme Court may order that the proceeding be determined by that court if it is satisfied that there are special
-- reasons for doing so in the particular circumstances of the proceeding other than reasons relevant to the convenience of
-- the parties." (4): before that order, notice to both Attorneys-General and a reasonable time.
-- (10): "This section does not apply to an appeal that is instituted in the Full Court of the Supreme Court of a State or
-- Territory if the court whose decision is the subject of the appeal had made an order under subsection (3) ..."
-- s 6A(3): where s 6A applies and both proceedings are in the same State, "neither subsection 5(1) nor section 6 applies to
-- require the Supreme Court to transfer".
DECLARE `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer so much of my proceeding as is within the Federal Court's jurisdiction (including accrued jurisdiction) to the Federal Court (s 6(1), (1A), (2)(a))`
    `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer so much of my proceeding as is within the receiving court's jurisdiction to whichever of the FCFCOA (Division 1), the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory it thinks appropriate (s 6(1), (1A), (2)(b))`
    `yes, but the text names no receiving court: the matter is a special federal matter under paragraph (aa), which s 6(2) does not mention`
    `no: the Supreme Court has ordered, for special reasons, that it determine the proceeding itself (s 6(3)); it can do so only after notice to both Attorneys-General and a reasonable time (s 6(4))`
    `no: section 6 does not apply to this appeal, because the court below made an order under s 6(3) (s 6(10))`
    `no: section 6A applies, so section 6 does not require the Supreme Court to transfer my federal matter proceeding; it may still do so if it considers that appropriate (s 6A(3))`
    `no: the matter is not a special federal matter (s 3(1))`
    `no: section 6 applies only to a proceeding pending in the Supreme Court of a State or Territory`

@export My civil proceeding is in a State or Territory Supreme Court and raises a federal question (competition law, judicial review of a Commonwealth decision, a question of law from a Commonwealth tribunal). Must it be transferred to the Federal Court?
GIVEN `the court my proceeding is in` IS A `Court` @desc The court where your proceeding is pending (the Family Court of Western Australia counts as Western Australia's Supreme Court here)
      `the kind of matter` IS A `Kind of matter` @desc Which kind of federal matter is in your proceeding, if any
      `my proceeding is in the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is in the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory (matters only for a section 60G Family Law Act matter)
      `a Commonwealth law specifically provides for this appeal, reference or case stated to a Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a question of law from a Commonwealth tribunal: whether a Commonwealth Act specifically provides for that appeal, reference or case stated to go to a State or Territory Supreme Court
      `the Supreme Court would have jurisdiction apart from this Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court could hear the matter without this Act
      `the Supreme Court has ordered that it determine the proceeding itself` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has made an order under section 6(3), for special reasons, that it will decide the proceeding itself
      `this is an appeal to the Full Court where the court below made a section 6(3) order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this is an appeal in the Full Court of the Supreme Court from a decision where the court below had ordered under section 6(3) that it decide the special federal matter
      `section 6A applies and the related State proceeding is in the same State` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether section 6A applies to your proceeding (see the section 6A question) and the related State proceeding is pending in a court of the same State
GIVETH A `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court`
DECIDE `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court question` IS
    IF (NOT (`is a Supreme Court of a State or Territory, read with s 3(2)` `the court my proceeding is in`))
    THEN `no: section 6 applies only to a proceeding pending in the Supreme Court of a State or Territory`
    ELSE IF (NOT (`is a special federal matter` `the kind of matter` `my proceeding is in the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory` `a Commonwealth law specifically provides for this appeal, reference or case stated to a Supreme Court` `the Supreme Court would have jurisdiction apart from this Act`))
         THEN `no: the matter is not a special federal matter (s 3(1))`
         ELSE IF `this is an appeal to the Full Court where the court below made a section 6(3) order`
              THEN `no: section 6 does not apply to this appeal, because the court below made an order under s 6(3) (s 6(10))`
              ELSE IF `section 6A applies and the related State proceeding is in the same State` AND (`the kind of matter` EQUALS `(c) the Administrative Decisions (Judicial Review) Act 1977` OR `the kind of matter` EQUALS `(e) the Federal Court's original jurisdiction under section 39B of the Judiciary Act 1903`)
                   THEN `no: section 6A applies, so section 6 does not require the Supreme Court to transfer my federal matter proceeding; it may still do so if it considers that appropriate (s 6A(3))`
                   ELSE IF `the Supreme Court has ordered that it determine the proceeding itself`
                        THEN `no: the Supreme Court has ordered, for special reasons, that it determine the proceeding itself (s 6(3)); it can do so only after notice to both Attorneys-General and a reasonable time (s 6(4))`
                        ELSE IF `the kind of matter` EQUALS `(ab) section 60G of the Family Law Act 1975 (leave for a prescribed adoption)`
                             THEN `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer so much of my proceeding as is within the receiving court's jurisdiction to whichever of the FCFCOA (Division 1), the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory it thinks appropriate (s 6(1), (1A), (2)(b))`
                             ELSE IF `the kind of matter` EQUALS `(aa) the Competition Code of the Australian Capital Territory or the Northern Territory`
                                  THEN `yes, but the text names no receiving court: the matter is a special federal matter under paragraph (aa), which s 6(2) does not mention`
                                  ELSE `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer so much of my proceeding as is within the Federal Court's jurisdiction (including accrued jurisdiction) to the Federal Court (s 6(1), (1A), (2)(a))`

§§ `s 6(4), (5) -- the notice to the Attorneys-General (only if the Supreme Court is asked to keep a special federal matter)`

-- s 6(4): "Before making an order under subsection (3), the court must be satisfied that: (a) a written notice specifying the
-- nature of the special federal matter has been given to the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth and the Attorney-General of
-- the State or Territory where the proceeding is pending; and (b) a reasonable time has elapsed since the giving of the
-- notice ..." (5): "For the purposes of subsection (4), the court: (a) may adjourn the proceeding for such time as the court
-- thinks necessary and may make such order as to costs in relation to an adjournment as it thinks fit; and (b) may direct a
-- party to the proceeding to give a notice in accordance with that subsection."
-- The Act names no one who must give the notice and sets no time for it. A party is bound only when the court directs it.
-- The direction's time limit is the court's, so it is an input (in days).
DECLARE `Notice party` IS ONE OF
    `I, the party the court directed`
    `the Supreme Court`

DECLARE `Notice act` IS ONE OF
    `give written notice of the nature of the special federal matter to the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth and the Attorney-General of the State or Territory`
    `order that the proceeding be determined by the Supreme Court`

GIVEN `the time the court allowed, in days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Notice party` `Notice act`
DECIDE `s 6(5)(b) -- I must give the notice the court directed` `the time the court allowed, in days` IS
    PARTY `I, the party the court directed`
    MUST  `give written notice of the nature of the special federal matter to the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth and the Attorney-General of the State or Territory`
    WITHIN `the time the court allowed, in days`
    HENCE
        PARTY `the Supreme Court`
        MAY   `order that the proceeding be determined by the Supreme Court`
    LEST  BREACH BY `I, the party the court directed` BECAUSE "s 6(5)(b): the court directed me to give the s 6(4) notice to both Attorneys-General and I did not give it in the time the court allowed"

DECLARE `Must I notify the Attorneys-General` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court has directed me to give written notice of the nature of the special federal matter to the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth and the Attorney-General of the State or Territory (s 6(4)(a), (5)(b))`
    `not unless the court directs me: the Act names no one who must give the notice, but the court cannot keep the matter until it is satisfied the notice was given and a reasonable time has passed (s 6(4))`
    `no: the notice is needed only where a special federal matter is pending in a Supreme Court that is asked to keep it (s 6(3), (4))`

@export My proceeding in a Supreme Court involves a special federal matter and someone wants the Supreme Court to keep it. Must I give notice to the Attorneys-General?
GIVEN `my proceeding in a Supreme Court involves a special federal matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding, pending in a State or Territory Supreme Court, involves a special federal matter (see the section 6 question)
      `the court has directed me to give the notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has directed you, under section 6(5)(b), to give the notice to the Attorneys-General
GIVETH A `Must I notify the Attorneys-General`
DECIDE `Must I notify the Attorneys-General question` IS
    IF (NOT `my proceeding in a Supreme Court involves a special federal matter`)
    THEN `no: the notice is needed only where a special federal matter is pending in a Supreme Court that is asked to keep it (s 6(3), (4))`
    ELSE IF `the court has directed me to give the notice`
         THEN `yes: the court has directed me to give written notice of the nature of the special federal matter to the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth and the Attorney-General of the State or Territory (s 6(4)(a), (5)(b))`
         ELSE `not unless the court directs me: the Act names no one who must give the notice, but the court cannot keep the matter until it is satisfied the notice was given and a reasonable time has passed (s 6(4))`

-- s 6(5)(a) (above): the court "may make such order as to costs in relation to an adjournment as it thinks fit". s 6(7): "The
-- Attorney-General may authorise the payment by the Commonwealth to a party of an amount in respect of costs arising out of
-- the adjournment of a proceeding under this section, under a corresponding provision of a law of a State or under this
-- section and under such a provision."
DECLARE `Can my adjournment costs be met` IS ONE OF
    `possibly: the court may order costs of the adjournment as it thinks fit (s 6(5)(a)), and the Commonwealth Attorney-General may authorise the Commonwealth to pay me an amount for my costs arising out of it (s 6(7)); both are discretions`
    `no: sections 6(5)(a) and 6(7) deal only with the costs of an adjournment under section 6 (or a corresponding State provision)`

@export My proceeding was adjourned so the Attorneys-General could be notified of a special federal matter. Can my costs of the adjournment be met?
GIVEN `my proceeding was adjourned under section 6 or a corresponding State provision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court adjourned your proceeding so that the Attorneys-General could consider a special federal matter (section 6 of this Act, or a corresponding section of a State cross-vesting Act)
GIVETH A `Can my adjournment costs be met`
DECIDE `Can my adjournment costs be met question` IS
    IF `my proceeding was adjourned under section 6 or a corresponding State provision`
    THEN `possibly: the court may order costs of the adjournment as it thinks fit (s 6(5)(a)), and the Commonwealth Attorney-General may authorise the Commonwealth to pay me an amount for my costs arising out of it (s 6(7)); both are discretions`
    ELSE `no: sections 6(5)(a) and 6(7) deal only with the costs of an adjournment under section 6 (or a corresponding State provision)`

-- s 6(8): "Nothing in this section prevents the court granting urgent relief of an interlocutory nature if it is in the
-- interests of justice to do so."
DECLARE `Can I still get urgent interim relief` IS ONE OF
    `yes: section 6 does not stop the Supreme Court granting me urgent interlocutory relief, as the court thinks the interests of justice require (s 6(8))`
    `section 6 does not stand in the way, but the Supreme Court has not found that the interests of justice call for urgent interlocutory relief (s 6(8))`

@export My proceeding in a Supreme Court involves a special federal matter that may have to go to the Federal Court. Can the Supreme Court still give me urgent interim relief?
GIVEN `the court thinks urgent interlocutory relief is in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court considers that urgent interim relief (such as an injunction until the matter is decided) is in the interests of justice
GIVETH A `Can I still get urgent interim relief`
DECIDE `Can I still get urgent interim relief question` IS
    IF `the court thinks urgent interlocutory relief is in the interests of justice`
    THEN `yes: section 6 does not stop the Supreme Court granting me urgent interlocutory relief, as the court thinks the interests of justice require (s 6(8))`
    ELSE `section 6 does not stand in the way, but the Supreme Court has not found that the interests of justice call for urgent interlocutory relief (s 6(8))`

-- s 6(9): "Where, through inadvertence, the Supreme Court of a State or Territory determines a proceeding of the kind mentioned
-- in subsection (1) without: (a) the court making an order under subsection (3) ...; or (b) a notice mentioned in subsection
-- (4) being given; nothing in this section invalidates the decision of that court."
-- s 7(8): "Where the Full Court of the Supreme Court of a State or Territory: (a) determines a proceeding to which subsection
-- (5) applies as mentioned in subsection (7); or (b) through inadvertence, determines a proceeding to which subsection (5)
-- applies; nothing in this section invalidates the decision of that court."
DECLARE `What went wrong` IS ONE OF
    `the Supreme Court, through inadvertence, decided a special federal matter without a s 6(3) order or the s 6(4) notice`
    `the Full Court of a Supreme Court decided an appeal that s 7(5) sends to a federal Full Court or the High Court (through inadvertence, or because the interests of justice required it to proceed)`
    `something else`

DECLARE `Is the decision invalid` IS ONE OF
    `no: nothing in section 6 invalidates the decision (s 6(9))`
    `no: nothing in section 7 invalidates the decision (s 7(8))`

@export A Supreme Court decided my case when this Act says it should have gone elsewhere. Is the decision invalid for that reason?
GIVEN `what went wrong` IS A `What went wrong` @desc What happened
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Is the decision invalid`
DECIDE `Is the decision invalid question` IS
    IF `what went wrong` EQUALS `the Supreme Court, through inadvertence, decided a special federal matter without a s 6(3) order or the s 6(4) notice`
    THEN `answered` `no: nothing in section 6 invalidates the decision (s 6(9))`
    ELSE IF `what went wrong` EQUALS `the Full Court of a Supreme Court decided an appeal that s 7(5) sends to a federal Full Court or the High Court (through inadvertence, or because the interests of justice required it to proceed)`
         THEN `answered` `no: nothing in section 7 invalidates the decision (s 7(8))`
         ELSE `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`

§§ `s 6A -- federal judicial review of a Commonwealth body acting under a State law`

-- s 6A(1): "This section applies to a proceeding (the federal matter proceeding) if: (a) a matter for determination in the
-- proceeding is covered by paragraph (c) or (e) of the definition of special federal matter in subsection 3(1); and (b) the
-- matter ... involves or relates to the exercise, or purported or proposed exercise, of functions or powers conferred on a
-- Commonwealth authority, or officer of the Commonwealth, by an enactment (the State enactment) referred to in paragraph (ca)
-- or (cb) of the definition of enactment in subsection 3(1) of the Administrative Decisions (Judicial Review) Act 1977; and
-- (c) the matter ... arises out of, or relates to, another proceeding (the State matter proceeding) pending in any court of
-- any State: (i) that arises, or a substantial part of which arises, under the State enactment or a corresponding enactment
-- of another State; and (ii) none of the matters for determination in which are covered by paragraph (c) or (e) ...;
-- regardless of which proceeding was commenced first."
-- (2): in the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1), the court "may transfer the proceeding to that Supreme Court" [of the
-- State where the State matter proceeding is pending] if, having regard to the interests of justice, it considers it
-- appropriate; "Subsection 5(4) does not apply to the federal matter proceeding." (3): in the Supreme Court of the same State,
-- neither s 5(1) nor s 6 requires transfer; the Supreme Court "may do so if it considers that to be appropriate".
-- s 6A(6): "Commonwealth authority means an authority or other body (whether incorporated or not) that is established or
-- continued in existence by or under an Act." "officer of the Commonwealth" as in s 75(v) of the Constitution (borrowed:
-- asked as a fact).
DECLARE `Where is my federal matter proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1)`
    `the Supreme Court of the State where the State matter proceeding is pending`
    `somewhere else`

DECLARE `What section 6A does for me` IS ONE OF
    `section 6A applies: the federal court may transfer my proceeding to the Supreme Court of the State where the related State proceeding is pending, if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice; s 5(4) does not apply (s 6A(2))`
    `section 6A applies: the Supreme Court is not required by s 5(1) or s 6 to transfer my proceeding to a federal court, but may do so if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice (s 6A(3))`
    `section 6A applies, but it changes nothing for a proceeding in that court; sections 5 and 6 apply as usual`
    `no: section 6A does not apply to my proceeding (s 6A(1))`

@export I am seeking judicial review (ADJR Act or Judiciary Act s 39B) of a Commonwealth body or officer acting under a State law, and a related State proceeding is pending. Does section 6A change where my case is heard?
GIVEN `my matter is under the ADJR Act or Judiciary Act s 39B` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a matter in your proceeding arises under the Administrative Decisions (Judicial Review) Act 1977, or is within the Federal Court's original jurisdiction under section 39B of the Judiciary Act 1903 (and the Supreme Court would not otherwise have jurisdiction over it)
      `it concerns a Commonwealth body or officer acting under a State enactment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it involves the exercise of functions or powers conferred on a Commonwealth authority or officer of the Commonwealth by a State enactment of the kind in paragraph (ca) or (cb) of the ADJR Act's definition of "enactment"
      `a related State proceeding under that State law is pending in a State court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your matter arises out of or relates to another proceeding, pending in any court of any State, that arises (wholly or substantially) under that State enactment or a corresponding one of another State
      `the State proceeding has no ADJR or s 39B matter in it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether none of the matters in that State proceeding arises under the ADJR Act or section 39B of the Judiciary Act 1903
      `where my proceeding is` IS A `Where is my federal matter proceeding` @desc Where your judicial review proceeding is pending
GIVETH A `What section 6A does for me`
DECIDE `What section 6A does for me question` IS
    IF (NOT (`my matter is under the ADJR Act or Judiciary Act s 39B` AND `it concerns a Commonwealth body or officer acting under a State enactment` AND `a related State proceeding under that State law is pending in a State court` AND `the State proceeding has no ADJR or s 39B matter in it`))
    THEN `no: section 6A does not apply to my proceeding (s 6A(1))`
    ELSE IF `where my proceeding is` EQUALS `the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1)`
         THEN `section 6A applies: the federal court may transfer my proceeding to the Supreme Court of the State where the related State proceeding is pending, if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice; s 5(4) does not apply (s 6A(2))`
         ELSE IF `where my proceeding is` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of the State where the State matter proceeding is pending`
              THEN `section 6A applies: the Supreme Court is not required by s 5(1) or s 6 to transfer my proceeding to a federal court, but may do so if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice (s 6A(3))`
              ELSE `section 6A applies, but it changes nothing for a proceeding in that court; sections 5 and 6 apply as usual`

§§ `s 7 -- where must I bring my appeal (only if I want to appeal)`

-- The Schedule (Section 7): Advance Australia Logo Protection Act 1984; Bankruptcy Act 1966; Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918;
-- Copyright Act 1968; Dental Benefits Act 2008; Designs Act 2003; Family Law Act 1975; Health Insurance Act 1973; Liquid Fuel
-- Emergency Act 1984; Patents Act 1990; Referendum (Machinery Provisions) Act 1984; Shipping Registration Act 1981; Trade
-- Marks Act 1955.
DECLARE `Scheduled Act` IS ONE OF
    `the Advance Australia Logo Protection Act 1984`
    `the Bankruptcy Act 1966`
    `the Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918`
    `the Copyright Act 1968`
    `the Dental Benefits Act 2008`
    `the Designs Act 2003`
    `the Family Law Act 1975`
    `the Health Insurance Act 1973`
    `the Liquid Fuel Emergency Act 1984`
    `the Patents Act 1990`
    `the Referendum (Machinery Provisions) Act 1984`
    `the Shipping Registration Act 1981`
    `the Trade Marks Act 1955`

-- Every Act named in the Schedule (used by the case file to show the list is closed at thirteen).
`the Schedule` MEANS LIST
    `the Advance Australia Logo Protection Act 1984`, `the Bankruptcy Act 1966`, `the Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918`,
    `the Copyright Act 1968`, `the Dental Benefits Act 2008`, `the Designs Act 2003`, `the Family Law Act 1975`,
    `the Health Insurance Act 1973`, `the Liquid Fuel Emergency Act 1984`, `the Patents Act 1990`,
    `the Referendum (Machinery Provisions) Act 1984`, `the Shipping Registration Act 1981`, `the Trade Marks Act 1955`

DECLARE `Decision I want to appeal` IS ONE OF
    `a single judge of the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1)`
    `a single judge of the Supreme Court of a State or Territory`
    `a judge of a State Family Court (the Family Court of Western Australia)`
    `a court of summary jurisdiction of a State (a magistrates or local court)`
    `some other court or tribunal`

-- s 7(1): "An appeal shall not be instituted from a decision of a single judge of the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit
-- and Family Court of Australia (Division 1) to the Full Court of the Supreme Court of a State or Territory." (2): "An appeal
-- shall not be instituted from the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1) to the
-- other of those courts."
-- (3): "Where it appears that the only matters for determination in a proceeding by way of an appeal from a decision of a
-- single judge of the Supreme Court of a State or Territory are matters other than matters arising under an Act specified in
-- the Schedule, that proceeding shall be instituted only in, and shall be determined only by, the Full Court of the Supreme
-- Court of that State or Territory."
-- (4): "An appeal shall not be instituted from a decision of a court of summary jurisdiction of a State to the Supreme Court
-- of the State if an appeal lies from that decision to the State Family Court of the State."
-- (5): "Subject to subsections (7) and (8), where it appears that a matter for determination in a proceeding by way of an
-- appeal from a decision of a single judge of the Supreme Court of a State or Territory (not being a proceeding to which
-- subsection (6) applies) is a matter arising under an Act specified in the Schedule, that proceeding shall be instituted
-- only in, and shall be determined only by: (a) the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Federal Circuit and Family Court
-- of Australia (Division 1), as the case requires; or (b) with special leave of the High Court, the High Court."
-- (6): "A proceeding by way of an appeal from a decision of a judge of a State Family Court, being a proceeding involving the
-- determination of: (a) a matter arising under an Act specified in the Schedule; and (b) another matter; may be dealt with as
-- if no matter for determination in the proceeding were a matter arising under an Act specified in the Schedule."
-- By s 3(2), "the Supreme Court of a State" in s 7(3) and (5) includes the State Family Court, so an appeal from a judge of the
-- Family Court of Western Australia is read with them.
DECLARE `Where must I appeal` IS ONE OF
    `only to the Full Court of the Supreme Court of that State or Territory (s 7(3))`
    `only to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the FCFCOA (Division 1), as the case requires, or, with special leave, the High Court (s 7(5))`
    `either as s 7(5) says (the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the FCFCOA (Division 1), or the High Court with special leave), or, because the appeal also involves another matter, it may be dealt with as if no Schedule Act matter were in it, that is by the Full Court of the State Family Court (s 7(6), (3))`
    `not to the Full Court of a State or Territory Supreme Court (s 7(1)), and not to the other of the two federal courts (s 7(2)); where it does lie is for the Act that created the court`
    `not to the Supreme Court of the State: an appeal lies to the State Family Court, so it goes there (s 7(4))`

@export I want to appeal a judge's decision in a civil case. Which court must I appeal to?
GIVEN `the decision` IS A `Decision I want to appeal` @desc Which court or judge made the decision you want to appeal
      `a matter in my appeal arises under an Act in the Schedule` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any matter in the appeal arises under one of these Acts: Advance Australia Logo Protection Act 1984, Bankruptcy Act 1966, Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918, Copyright Act 1968, Dental Benefits Act 2008, Designs Act 2003, Family Law Act 1975, Health Insurance Act 1973, Liquid Fuel Emergency Act 1984, Patents Act 1990, Referendum (Machinery Provisions) Act 1984, Shipping Registration Act 1981, Trade Marks Act 1955
      `my appeal also involves another matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the appeal also involves a matter that does not arise under one of those Acts
      `an appeal lies from the decision to the State Family Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a magistrates or local court decision: whether an appeal from it lies to the State Family Court (the Family Court of Western Australia)
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Where must I appeal`
DECIDE `Where must I appeal question` IS
    IF `the decision` EQUALS `a single judge of the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1)`
    THEN `answered` `not to the Full Court of a State or Territory Supreme Court (s 7(1)), and not to the other of the two federal courts (s 7(2)); where it does lie is for the Act that created the court`
    ELSE IF `the decision` EQUALS `a court of summary jurisdiction of a State (a magistrates or local court)`
         THEN IF `an appeal lies from the decision to the State Family Court`
              THEN `answered` `not to the Supreme Court of the State: an appeal lies to the State Family Court, so it goes there (s 7(4))`
              ELSE `not covered` `this Act does not say where this appeal lies: that is for the Act that created the court`
         ELSE IF `the decision` EQUALS `some other court or tribunal`
              THEN `not covered` `this Act does not say where this appeal lies: that is for the Act that created the court`
              ELSE IF (NOT `a matter in my appeal arises under an Act in the Schedule`)
                   THEN `answered` `only to the Full Court of the Supreme Court of that State or Territory (s 7(3))`
                   ELSE IF `the decision` EQUALS `a judge of a State Family Court (the Family Court of Western Australia)` AND `my appeal also involves another matter`
                        THEN `answered` `either as s 7(5) says (the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the FCFCOA (Division 1), or the High Court with special leave), or, because the appeal also involves another matter, it may be dealt with as if no Schedule Act matter were in it, that is by the Full Court of the State Family Court (s 7(6), (3))`
                        ELSE `answered` `only to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the FCFCOA (Division 1), as the case requires, or, with special leave, the High Court (s 7(5))`

-- s 7(7): "Where: (a) the Full Court of the Supreme Court of a State or Territory commences to hear a proceeding by way of an
-- appeal; and (b) before the Court determines the proceeding, it appears to the Court that the proceeding is a proceeding to
-- which subsection (5) applies; the Court shall, unless the interests of justice require that the Court proceed to determine
-- the proceeding, transfer the proceeding to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of
-- Australia (Division 1), as the case requires."
DECLARE `What happens to my appeal in the wrong Full Court` IS ONE OF
    `the Full Court must transfer my appeal to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the FCFCOA (Division 1), as the case requires (s 7(7))`
    `the Full Court may go on to decide my appeal, because the interests of justice require it (s 7(7)); its decision is not invalid for that (s 7(8))`
    `section 7(7) does not arise: my appeal is not one that s 7(5) sends to a federal Full Court`

@export My appeal has started in the Full Court of a State or Territory Supreme Court, but it involves a matter under an Act in the Schedule. What happens to it?
GIVEN `my appeal is one s 7(5) applies to` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your appeal is from a single judge of a Supreme Court and a matter in it arises under an Act in the Schedule (see the appeal question), and it is not a State Family Court appeal dealt with under section 7(6)
      `the Full Court thinks the interests of justice require it to proceed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Full Court considers that the interests of justice require it to go on and decide the appeal itself
GIVETH A `What happens to my appeal in the wrong Full Court`
DECIDE `What happens to my appeal in the wrong Full Court question` IS
    IF (NOT `my appeal is one s 7(5) applies to`)
    THEN `section 7(7) does not arise: my appeal is not one that s 7(5) sends to a federal Full Court`
    ELSE IF `the Full Court thinks the interests of justice require it to proceed`
         THEN `the Full Court may go on to decide my appeal, because the interests of justice require it (s 7(7)); its decision is not invalid for that (s 7(8))`
         ELSE `the Full Court must transfer my appeal to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the FCFCOA (Division 1), as the case requires (s 7(7))`

§§ `s 8 -- removal to an external Territory's Supreme Court (only for a case in an external Territory's lower court or tribunal)`

-- s 8(1): "Where: (a) a proceeding ... is pending in: (i) a court, other than the Supreme Court, of a Territory; or (ii) a
-- tribunal established by or under a law of a Territory; and (b) it appears to the Supreme Court of that Territory that: (i)
-- the relevant proceeding arises out of, or is related to, another proceeding pending in the Federal Court, the Federal
-- Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1), the Supreme Court of a State or the Supreme Court of another Territory
-- and, if an order is made under this subsection in relation to the relevant proceeding, there would be grounds on which that
-- other proceeding could be transferred to the Supreme Court of that first-mentioned Territory; or (ii) an order should be
-- made under this subsection in relation to the relevant proceeding so that consideration can be given to whether the
-- relevant proceeding should be transferred to another court; the Supreme Court of that first-mentioned Territory may, on the
-- application of a party to the relevant proceeding or of its own motion, make an order removing the relevant proceeding to
-- that Supreme Court." (2): the Act then applies as if the proceeding were pending in that Supreme Court. (3): the Supreme
-- Court "may, if the court considers it appropriate to do so, remit the proceeding to the court or tribunal from which the
-- proceeding was removed." A "Territory" here is an external Territory: s 3(1) excludes the ACT and the NT.
DECLARE `Can my case be removed to the Territory Supreme Court` IS ONE OF
    `yes, it may be: the Territory's Supreme Court may order my proceeding removed to it, on my application or its own motion; the Act then treats it as pending there, and the Supreme Court may later remit it (s 8(1)-(3))`
    `no: section 8 lets the Supreme Court remove a proceeding only where one of its grounds appears to it`
    `no: section 8 is only for a proceeding in a lower court or tribunal of an external Territory (the ACT and the NT are States under this Act)`

@export My case is in a lower court or tribunal of an external Territory (Norfolk Island, Christmas Island, Cocos (Keeling) Islands). Can it be removed to that Territory's Supreme Court?
GIVEN `my proceeding is in a lower court or tribunal of an external Territory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is pending in a court (other than the Supreme Court) or a tribunal of an external Territory
      `the Supreme Court thinks a related case elsewhere could then come to it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to that Territory's Supreme Court that your proceeding is related to one pending in the Federal Court, the FCFCOA (Division 1) or another Supreme Court, and that if yours were removed there would be grounds to transfer that one to it
      `the Supreme Court thinks removal is needed to consider a transfer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to that Supreme Court that your proceeding should be removed so that a transfer to another court can be considered
GIVETH A `Can my case be removed to the Territory Supreme Court`
DECIDE `Can my case be removed to the Territory Supreme Court question` IS
    IF (NOT `my proceeding is in a lower court or tribunal of an external Territory`)
    THEN `no: section 8 is only for a proceeding in a lower court or tribunal of an external Territory (the ACT and the NT are States under this Act)`
    ELSE IF `the Supreme Court thinks a related case elsewhere could then come to it` OR `the Supreme Court thinks removal is needed to consider a transfer`
         THEN `yes, it may be: the Territory's Supreme Court may order my proceeding removed to it, on my application or its own motion; the Act then treats it as pending there, and the Supreme Court may later remit it (s 8(1)-(3))`
         ELSE `no: section 8 lets the Supreme Court remove a proceeding only where one of its grounds appears to it`

§§ `s 10 -- sending an Australian Consumer Law claim to a lower court`

-- s 10: "Where: (a) a proceeding is pending in the Federal Court, the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division
-- 1) or the Supreme Court of a State or Territory; (b) a matter for determination in the proceeding is a matter arising under
-- Part 2-2, 3-1, 3-3 or 3-4 of Schedule 2 to the Competition and Consumer Act 2010, as that Part applies as a law of the
-- Commonwealth; (c) no matter for determination in the proceeding is a special federal matter; (d) the proceeding is not a
-- proceeding by way of an appeal from a judgment of a court; and (e) a court of a State or Territory, other than the Supreme
-- Court of that State or Territory, has jurisdiction with respect to all of the matters for determination in the proceeding;
-- the court referred to in paragraph (a) may, on the application of a party to the proceeding or of its own motion, transfer
-- the proceeding to the court referred to in paragraph (e)." Part 2-2 is unconscionable conduct; Part 3-1 unfair practices;
-- Part 3-3 safety of consumer goods and product-related services; Part 3-4 information standards.
DECLARE `Can my consumer claim go to a lower court` IS ONE OF
    `yes, it may: the court may transfer my proceeding to that lower court, on my application or its own motion (s 10)`
    `no: section 10 allows the transfer only if every one of its conditions is met`

@export My claim under the Australian Consumer Law (unconscionable conduct, unfair practices, consumer goods safety, information standards) is in a federal court or a Supreme Court. Can it be sent to a lower State or Territory court?
GIVEN `the court my proceeding is in` IS A `Court` @desc The court where your proceeding is pending
      `a matter arises under Part 2-2, 3-1, 3-3 or 3-4 of the Australian Consumer Law as a Commonwealth law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a matter in your proceeding arises under Part 2-2 (unconscionable conduct), 3-1 (unfair practices), 3-3 (safety of consumer goods) or 3-4 (information standards) of the Australian Consumer Law, as it applies as a law of the Commonwealth
      `a matter in it is a special federal matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any matter in your proceeding is a special federal matter (see the section 6 question)
      `it is an appeal from a court's judgment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is an appeal from a judgment of a court
      `a lower court has jurisdiction over every matter in it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a State or Territory court below the Supreme Court (for example a district, county, magistrates or local court) has jurisdiction over all the matters in your proceeding
GIVETH A `Can my consumer claim go to a lower court`
DECIDE `Can my consumer claim go to a lower court question` IS
    IF      (`is one of the two federal courts` `the court my proceeding is in` OR `is a Supreme Court of a State or Territory, read with s 3(2)` `the court my proceeding is in`)
        AND `a matter arises under Part 2-2, 3-1, 3-3 or 3-4 of the Australian Consumer Law as a Commonwealth law`
        AND (NOT `a matter in it is a special federal matter`)
        AND (NOT `it is an appeal from a court's judgment`)
        AND `a lower court has jurisdiction over every matter in it`
    THEN `yes, it may: the court may transfer my proceeding to that lower court, on my application or its own motion (s 10)`
    ELSE `no: section 10 allows the transfer only if every one of its conditions is met`

§§ `s 11 -- which law the court applies to my claim`

-- s 11(1): "Where it appears to a court that the court will, or will be likely to, in determining a matter for determination
-- in a proceeding, be exercising jurisdiction conferred by this Act or by a law of a State relating to cross-vesting of
-- jurisdiction: (a) subject to paragraphs (b) and (c), the court shall, in determining that matter, apply the law in force in
-- the State or Territory in which the court is sitting (including choice of law rules); (b) subject to paragraph (c), if that
-- matter is a right of action arising under a written law of another State or Territory, the court shall, in determining
-- that matter, apply the written and unwritten law of that other State or Territory; and (c) the rules of evidence and
-- procedure to be applied in dealing with that matter shall be such as the court considers appropriate in the
-- circumstances, being rules that are applied in a superior court in Australia or in an external Territory."
-- (2): for the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1), "the State or Territory in which the court is sitting" is "the State
-- or Territory in which any matter for determination in the proceeding was first commenced in or transferred to that court".
DECLARE `Which law applies to my claim` IS ONE OF
    `the written and unwritten law of the other State or Territory under whose written law my right of action arises (s 11(1)(b)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
    `the law in force in the State or Territory where the court sits, including its choice of law rules (s 11(1)(a)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
    `the law in force, including its choice of law rules, in the State or Territory where a matter in my proceeding was first commenced in or transferred to the federal court (s 11(1)(a), (2)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`

@export My claim is being heard by a court using cross-vested jurisdiction (for example after a transfer from another State). Which State's law will the court apply?
GIVEN `the court is exercising cross-vested jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the court that, in deciding your matter, it will or is likely to be exercising jurisdiction conferred by this Act or by a State or Territory cross-vesting Act
      `my right of action arises under a written law of another State or Territory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is a right of action created by an Act or other written law of a State or Territory other than the one where the court sits (for example a statutory compensation claim under another State's Act)
      `the court is the Federal Court or the FCFCOA` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court hearing the matter is the Federal Court of Australia or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1)
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Which law applies to my claim`
DECIDE `Which law applies to my claim question` IS
    IF (NOT `the court is exercising cross-vested jurisdiction`)
    THEN `not covered` `the court is not exercising jurisdiction conferred by this Act or by a State cross-vesting law, so s 11 does not say which law it applies`
    ELSE IF `my right of action arises under a written law of another State or Territory`
         THEN `answered` `the written and unwritten law of the other State or Territory under whose written law my right of action arises (s 11(1)(b)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
         ELSE IF `the court is the Federal Court or the FCFCOA`
              THEN `answered` `the law in force, including its choice of law rules, in the State or Territory where a matter in my proceeding was first commenced in or transferred to the federal court (s 11(1)(a), (2)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
              ELSE `answered` `the law in force in the State or Territory where the court sits, including its choice of law rules (s 11(1)(a)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`

§§ `ss 11(3), 12, 13 -- after a transfer (only if my proceeding has been transferred or removed)`

-- s 11(3): "Where a proceeding is transferred or removed to a court ... from another court ..., the transferee court shall
-- deal with the proceeding as if, subject to any order of the transferee court, the steps that had been taken for the
-- purposes of the proceeding in the transferor court (including the making of an order), or similar steps, had been taken in
-- the transferee court."
-- s 12: "Where a proceeding is transferred or removed to a court, that court may make an order as to costs that relate to the
-- conduct of the proceeding before the transfer or removal if those costs have not already been dealt with by another court."
DECLARE `What carries over after transfer` IS ONE OF
    `the steps already taken (including orders made) count as taken in the new court, unless it orders otherwise (s 11(3)); and the new court may order costs of the proceeding before the transfer (s 12)`
    `the steps already taken (including orders made) count as taken in the new court, unless it orders otherwise (s 11(3)); the costs before the transfer have already been dealt with by another court, so s 12 gives the new court no power over them`

@export My proceeding has been transferred or removed to another court. Do I have to start again, and who deals with the costs so far?
GIVEN `the earlier court has already dealt with the costs before transfer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another court has already made an order about the costs of the proceeding before it was transferred or removed
GIVETH A `What carries over after transfer`
DECIDE `What carries over after transfer question` IS
    IF `the earlier court has already dealt with the costs before transfer`
    THEN `the steps already taken (including orders made) count as taken in the new court, unless it orders otherwise (s 11(3)); the costs before the transfer have already been dealt with by another court, so s 12 gives the new court no power over them`
    ELSE `the steps already taken (including orders made) count as taken in the new court, unless it orders otherwise (s 11(3)); and the new court may order costs of the proceeding before the transfer (s 12)`

-- s 13: "An appeal does not lie from a decision of a court: (a) in relation to the transfer or removal of a proceeding under
-- this Act; or (b) as to which rules of evidence and procedure are to be applied pursuant to subsection 11(1)."
DECLARE `Decision about my proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `a decision to transfer or remove my proceeding under this Act, or not to`
    `a decision about which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1)`
    `some other decision`

DECLARE `Can I appeal that decision` IS ONE OF
    `no: an appeal does not lie from a decision about the transfer or removal of a proceeding under this Act (s 13(a))`
    `no: an appeal does not lie from a decision about which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1) (s 13(b))`

@export Can I appeal against the court's decision to transfer (or not transfer) my proceeding, or about the rules of evidence and procedure it will apply?
GIVEN `the decision` IS A `Decision about my proceeding` @desc Which decision you want to appeal
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can I appeal that decision`
DECIDE `Can I appeal that decision question` IS
    IF `the decision` EQUALS `a decision to transfer or remove my proceeding under this Act, or not to`
    THEN `answered` `no: an appeal does not lie from a decision about the transfer or removal of a proceeding under this Act (s 13(a))`
    ELSE IF `the decision` EQUALS `a decision about which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1)`
         THEN `answered` `no: an appeal does not lie from a decision about which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1) (s 13(b))`
         ELSE `not covered` `this Act does not say where this appeal lies: that is for the Act that created the court`

§§ `s 14 -- enforcing my judgment (only if I have a judgment)`

-- s 14(1): "A judgment of the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1) that is given, in
-- whole or in part, in the exercise of jurisdiction conferred by a law or laws relating to cross-vesting of jurisdiction is
-- enforceable in a Territory as if the judgment had been given entirely in the exercise of the jurisdiction of that court
-- apart from any such law." (2): "A judgment of the Supreme Court of a Territory that is given, in whole or in part, in the
-- exercise of jurisdiction conferred by a law or laws relating to cross-vesting of jurisdiction is enforceable in that
-- Territory as if the judgment had been given entirely in the exercise of the jurisdiction of that court apart from any such
-- law." s 3(1): "judgment means a judgment, decree or order, whether final or interlocutory."
DECLARE `Can I enforce my judgment in the Territory` IS ONE OF
    `yes: my federal court judgment is enforceable in an external Territory as if given entirely in the court's own jurisdiction (s 14(1))`
    `yes: my Territory Supreme Court judgment is enforceable in that Territory as if given entirely in the court's own jurisdiction (s 14(2))`

@export I have a judgment given (wholly or partly) under cross-vested jurisdiction. Can I enforce it in an external Territory?
GIVEN `the court that gave my judgment` IS A `Court` @desc The court that gave the judgment (a judgment includes any decree or order, final or interim)
      `it was given wholly or partly under cross-vested jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the judgment was given, in whole or in part, in jurisdiction conferred by this Act or a State or Territory cross-vesting Act
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can I enforce my judgment in the Territory`
DECIDE `Can I enforce my judgment in the Territory question` IS
    IF `it was given wholly or partly under cross-vested jurisdiction` AND `is one of the two federal courts` `the court that gave my judgment`
    THEN `answered` `yes: my federal court judgment is enforceable in an external Territory as if given entirely in the court's own jurisdiction (s 14(1))`
    ELSE IF `it was given wholly or partly under cross-vested jurisdiction` AND `the court that gave my judgment` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of an external Territory`
         THEN `answered` `yes: my Territory Supreme Court judgment is enforceable in that Territory as if given entirely in the court's own jurisdiction (s 14(2))`
         ELSE `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`
